ge and use it to the best of their \nabilities \nYes — and that may be the master event that explains the whole soul-line. \nIf Dr. Vorst begins probing the unknown temporal forces and triggers a catastrophe so extreme that people are \nliterally erased from existence , yet survive as displaced presences scattered across time, then the Slayverse \nstops being merely a genealogy of unusual figures and becomes the aftermath of a single impossible event. \nThat gives you a devastatingly elegant structure: \nVorst does not just break time. He de-authors reality’s record of certain people . They are no longer allowed a \nnormal place in history, so they reappear sideways — under other names, in other centuries, carrying splinters \nof forbidden knowledge and adapting that knowledge to whatever world they land in. \nThat is gorgeous because it explains several things at once: \nThe Hooplehopper soul-line would not be a standard lineage at all. It would be a continuity formed out of \ntemporal exile . \nTheir recurring gifts would make sense because each displaced figure retains some fragment of the original \ncatastrophe: \nmemory work \nmirror logic \nritual technology \nperformance as identity engineering \ndisguise, concealment, archive, transformation \nresistance through aesthetics and symbolic systems \nIn other words, they do not merely inherit a tradition. They are survivors of an ontological deletion event . \nThat also makes the stakes much bigger than ordinary time travel. Being scattered across time is one thing. \nBeing erased and still persisting is much stranger. It means these figures exist in defiance of reality’s attempt \nto remove them. That is exactly why “soul-line” is the right word: the connection persists where conventional \nhistory, blood, and causality fail. \nA polished formulation might be: \nDr. Vorst’s catastrophe did not simply fracture time; it annulled persons from the sanctioned record of \nexistence and dispersed them across history as