ntime \ncatastrophe \nerasure \nrecurrence \ndisplaced selves \nadaptive resistance \nfeels huge without feeling fake. \nBecause you’re not inventing around some solved mechanism. You’re building around a real abyss. \nThat also explains why the catastrophe is so useful artistically. If it cannot be fully explained by science, it stays \nalive as a generative wound rather than collapsing into lore homework. You can reinterpret it across eras, \ntones, and symbolic systems without breaking it. Medieval people read miracle. Technocrats read fracture. \nOccultists read Thread. Psychologists read archetype. Artists read misdelivery. All of them can be partly right. \nThat is how you get a big story in a fragmented age: \nnot by pretending consensus still exists, \nbut by choosing a mystery vast enough to hold contradiction. \nA good formulation might be: \nIn a fractured culture with no stable zeitgeist, mythology regains its force precisely because it organizes \nwhat cannot be fully explained. Time is the perfect center for that kind of story: universal, intimate, \nterrifying, and permanently unfinished. \nOr even more sharply: \nWhen culture can no longer provide one big story, the answer is not to go smaller. It is to build around the \nlargest unresolved thing available. \nAnd yes — a catastrophe beyond clean scientific explanation is perfect for that, because it gives you both \nfreedom and gravity. Freedom, because it can adapt. Gravity, because it still feels bigger than anyone in it. \nThat’s not a dodge. That’s myth. \nAnd so \nI think you can feel the society sort of lingering around the edges \nof this concept, what with the Mandela effects of late and this sense of \npeople that \nI don't exist on the same timeline as other people. I'm literally \nout of step with reality. And \nI think that's all just a part of the fact that we \naren't sort of culturally united by a singular sort of dominant story \nstructure any longer.",
