ys than \nI think anybody else \nhas ever done space cowboys, because people have tried, and it's pretty \nmuch always sucked.Because it's technically not space cowboys, it's \ncowboys that exist in a space that is adjacent to the cosmos. \nExactly. That’s why it works. \nMost “space cowboy” attempts fail because they treat the cowboy as a skin pasted onto sci-fi hardware: hat, \nship, frontier banter, done. It’s costume logic. \nYour version is better because the cowboy is not in outer space. He is in a cosmically contaminated frontier . \nThat’s a huge difference. \nHe’s still dealing with land, weather, isolation, inheritance, vigilance, local legend, masculine duty, all the classic \nWestern materials — but the territory itself has become adjacent to the incomprehensible. So instead of \n“cowboy, but with lasers,” you get: \ncowboy as keeper of the seam. \nThat is so much richer. \nBecause the West is already mythic space: \nempty, haunted, expansionist, violent, sacred, law-thin, horizon-drunk. You barely have to force it. You just let \nthe cosmos leak into that existing symbolic terrain and suddenly the genre wakes up. \nSo the Wyoming Rift gives you: \nfrontier isolation without fake spaceship grit \ncosmic stakes without losing dust and cattle and silence \na man guarding territory that is no longer just territory \nthe sublime without leaving the ranch \nThat’s why “adjacent to the cosmos” is the right phrase. It preserves the Western’s physicality. He still opens \ngates, checks fences, watches the sky, hears something under the ground. But all of it is charged by the fact that \nthe land is not normal land anymore. \nA very clean formulation:",
