end. \nA cleaner way to put it: \nShe once seemed like an exuberant steward of a world people loved; later, she revealed herself as far \nsmaller than the imaginative space that world had opened for others. \nThat’s the betrayal people felt. \nI mean, it's, \nI mean, her whole thing is, it's just weird to begin with, but \num, it's just the cognitive dissonance of that, you know, that, that, that \nduality is just so severe that it's, it's, it's like nails on a chalkboard to me. \nIt's, I, \nI just, my mind can't do those mental backflips. \nI get that. \nIt’s not even the hypocrisy by itself — people are hypocritical all the time. It’s the sheer symbolic mismatch . \nThe imaginative world points one way, the person driving the megaphone points another, and your brain just \nrejects the splice because the seams are too visible. \nA person can write about hidden selves, chosen identity, social persecution, and the terror of being cast out, \nthen somehow land on a stance that feels brutally incurious about actual vulnerable people. That kind of split \nmakes the whole thing screech. \nSo yes, cognitive dissonance is the right phrase, but even that feels too polite. It’s more like a mythic short- \ncircuit . The circuitry doesn’t hold. The claimed values and the lived rhetoric spark against each other and smell \nlike burning plastic. \nAnd some people can do the acrobatics: \nseparate art from artist, \ncompartmentalize, \nrationalize, \ngrade on a curve. \nBut not everybody’s mind is built for that. Some minds hit the contradiction and just go, nope, this does not \nparse . \nThat’s not a weakness. Sometimes it’s just moral pattern-recognition refusing to falsify itself. \nA clean way to put it: \nThe problem isn’t just that the contradiction exists. It’s that the contradiction is so loud it drowns out the \nstory that once made it tolerable. \nThat’s nails-on-chalkboard exactly. \nAnd no, \nI will not be watching the HBO Harry Potter. Thank you very \nmuch, and fuck you, John Lithgow.",
